// FNV-1a over the bytes of a string, folded into a non-negative int.
//
// A hash is only useful if it is stable, so this one is defined entirely by its
// own arithmetic and not by anything the runtime does: 2166136261 is FNV's
// offset basis (written as `0 - 2128831035`, which is its 32-bit two's
// complement, because the literal itself is past the int range) and 16777619 is
// FNV's prime. The multiply wraps, which is what a 32-bit FNV is supposed to do.
//
// The sign bit is masked off on the way out, so the answer is 0..2147483647 and
// `str_hash(s) % nbuckets` is a bucket index rather than a negative one. That
// costs one bit of the hash and saves every caller a fixup.
//
// The parenthesis around `hv ^ c` is load-bearing: **bitwise binds tighter than
// comparison in this language but looser than `*`**, so `hv ^ c * 16777619`
// would multiply first.

str_hash(string s) {
  int hv = str_hash_run(s, 0 - 2128831035, 0);
} return int hv & 2147483647;
(""):(18652613)
("hello"):(1335831723)

str_hash_run(string s, int hv, int i) {
  while (charat(s, i) >= 0) {
    hv = (hv ^ charat(s, i)) * 16777619;
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return int hv;
("", -2128831035, 0):(-2128831035)
("a", -2128831035, 0):(-468965076)
